  "post": {
    "caption": "Cartier's Crash watch wasn't in a crash. Cartier London introduced the Crash in 1967. Jean Jacques Cartier developed it with the designer Rupert Emmerson, and they pinched and bent an oval watch. A popular story says a customer's watch warped in a car crash and Cartier copied the damage. Sotheby's calls that story an urban myth. The melted look recalls a Dali painting, but no link is proven. The first London Crash had a gold case and a Jaeger LeCoultre movement. Cartier Paris began making the Crash in 1991.",
